  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a distinct cluster of symptoms: regular headaches/migraines, distorted eyesight, excessive appetite, a painful neck, melancholy/irritability, and acid reflux/indigestion. Step-by-step analysis shows this exact combination maps directly to Reference Case [1], which describes 'frequent headaches, stomach acid, indigestion, hazy and distorted vision, increased appetite, stiff neck, depression, irritation, and visual distortion' and diagnoses it as migraine. \n\nWhile individual symptoms like acid reflux and indigestion could point to gastroesophageal reflux disease or peptic ulcer disease, and a painful neck could suggest cervical spondylosis, those conditions require their own specific hallmark presentations (e.g., burning stomach pain worsening with food for PUD, prominent heartburn/sore throat for GERD, back pain/cough/stiffness for cervical spondylosis), none of which are present. Excessive appetite alone does not establish diabetes without classic signs like polyuria, polydipsia, or fatigue. Because the full constellation of symptoms uniquely identifies the migraine syndrome described in the references, and the answer shape requires a single unit, migraine is the only factually supported diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]migraine[/DIAGNOSIS]"
